course: dictionary
lesson: 514
title: Assistance: help from people and machines
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Assistance means help or support given to someone. When you meet it in a sentence, ask who is being helped and who is giving the help. In «Do you need assistance?», someone is offering help.
explain: Assistance is a more formal word than help. You will often see it on signs and in announcements, like «call for assistance». You can still say help with your friends, but assistance is the word used in official places.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «A teaching assistant» is a person who helps a teacher and the pupils in a class, so the job is to help. «Roadside assistance» is a service that comes to help when your car breaks down.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping in help. «Do you need help?» still makes sense, so assistance means help. If your swap sounds wrong, read the sentence again and look for more clues.
fact: Assistance means help or support given to someone.
fact: Assistance is often a formal word for help from a service, such as medical assistance.
fact: «Roadside assistance» means help from a service when your car breaks down.
fact: «A teaching assistant» helps a teacher and pupils who need extra help in class.
fact: An assistance dog in a jacket marked «do not distract» is working, so do not stroke, call or feed it without asking its owner.
exercise: A shop worker asks, «Do you need assistance?» What does assistance mean? | A map of the town | A small animal | *Help | A loud noise
exercise: «Driver assistance, like automatic emergency braking, helps the driver.» What is driver assistance? | A machine that replaces the driver | *Help that supports the driver | A type of car horn | Paint on the road
exercise: In «call for assistance», which word names the help? | *assistance | call | for
test: What does «came to her assistance» mean? | Ran away from her | A bike ride to her house | *Came to help her | Forgot her name
test: «An assistance dog in a jacket marked "do not distract" is working.» What should you do? | Stroke it to say hello | *Leave it alone and do not distract it | Call it over with a treat | Feed it a biscuit
exam: Which sentence uses assistance as a formal word for help from a service? | «The cat slept on the mat.» | «We went to the park after lunch.» | «Please pass the salt.» | *«Roadside assistance will come within an hour.»
